{
  "description": "Shared 8-bit display oracle for the server windowing pipeline (src/pixels/window.rs, src/pixels/render.rs) and the client raw-frame renderer (frontend/src/lib/rawWindowing.ts). Stored samples are one row of unsigned 16-bit values. Expected outputs follow PS3.3 C.11.2.1.2.1 LINEAR windowing of the rescaled values, rounded half up; MONOCHROME1 is 255 minus that quantized output (INVERSE presentation, C.11.6.1.2); Pixel Padding is excluded from automatic windows and drawn black. Window precedence: mode full_dynamic, then explicit wc/ww, then the DICOM window, then the 1st/99th percentile of the frame. A case with `real_world` windows that mapping's values instead (the server with `unit`, the client with the mapping): stored values go through the mapping (a LUT indexed from first_value_mapped, or slope and intercept), the window follows the LINEAR function without the integer half-unit offsets, clamped to [0, 1] and rounded half up, and stored values outside [first_value_mapped, last_value_mapped] take the window's low end before any MONOCHROME1 inversion. A case with `shutter` (a rectangular opening over the row's one-based columns and its Shutter Presentation Value) or `overlay` (one overlay bit per sample) also lists `layer`: the presentation layer, gray where the shutter fill (then overlay graphics, 255) replaces the windowed value and null where it is transparent; the server draws it into the display frame, and the client composites it over the raw render. `modality_lut` and `voi_lut` (`first_value_mapped`, `bits`, `values`) declare a Modality LUT, which replaces the rescale, and a VOI LUT, which presents Modality values in default mode when no window is requested or stored (index truncated then clamped to the table, output scaled to 8 bits as (v * 255 + max / 2) / max in integers); the client reads both from the frame's value mapping. `sample` names another stored type than unsigned 16-bit: `int32` (signed), `float32` (Float Pixel Data, with Float Pixel Padding), or `float64` (Double Float Pixel Data); such samples go through the same pipeline one at a time, automatic windows sorting every Modality value. Float samples, fractional rescale values and real-world windows use the continuous function (sample - (center - width/2))/width with no width floor for positive spans; integer Modality samples retain LINEAR half-unit boundaries and a width floor of 1. Constant automatic Modality windows use width 1.",
